--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Multi-argument std::function construction and call.  Layer-1 partial-spec pack
deduction (cpp11_partial_spec_pack_after_fixed), the member function-pointer
pack-arity collapse (cpp11_variadic_pack_in_member_funptr_type) and the
method-body pack-expansion use (cpp11_variadic_pack_in_method_body_call) are all
fixed, so the converting constructor resolves, wires up _M_invoker / _M_manager
with the correct arity, and operator() forwards both arguments
(`_M_invoker(_M_functor, std::forward<_ArgTypes>(__args)...)`) correctly to the
handler.  The remaining gap is deeper in the dispatch chain
(_Function_handler::_M_invoke -> std::__invoke_r -> __invoke_impl ->
*_M_get_pointer(functor)): the invocation yields a wrong result (the call
returns 1 instead of 5 for add(2,3)) although the operator()-level argument
forwarding is correct.  Single-argument std::function invokes correctly and
soundly (N5008 [func.wrap.func], [temp.variadic]/5).  Previously this test passed
only VACUOUSLY ("found no match for symbol 'function'").  Flip to CORE once the
multi-argument invoke/__invoke_r dispatch returns the correct result.
